# Trivy ignore file for everything under images/. Suppresses findings
# that are genuinely not ours to fix - see the per-entry comments below
# for why each one is here.
#
# HOW THIS GETS LOADED: trivy's own --ignorefile flag defaults to the
# literal string ".trivyignore" resolved against the current working
# directory (confirmed via `trivy image --help` on v0.65.0: "default
# \".trivyignore\""). .github/workflows/ci-build-scan.yaml checks the
# repo out to $GITHUB_WORKSPACE and runs aquasecurity/trivy-action@v0.33.1
# from there with no working-directory override, so trivy finds this file
# on its own - no `trivyignores:` input needed in the workflow, and none
# is set. Confirmed by reading trivy-action's own entrypoint.sh at that
# tag: it only ever sets TRIVY_IGNOREFILE when the action's `trivyignores:`
# input is non-empty; left unset (as here), trivy falls through to its
# own CLI default and picks this file up unassisted.
#
# WHY PLAIN .trivyignore AND NOT .trivyignore.yaml: yaml support (with
# `purls`/`paths` scoping and an `expired_at` field) is real in trivy
# v0.65.0, but two things rule it out here:
#   1. Trivy decides yaml-vs-plain purely by filepath.Ext() of the path
#      passed to --ignorefile (pkg/result/ignore.go, ParseIgnoreFile) -
#      it never sniffs content. Yaml mode also requires passing
#      --ignorefile explicitly; trivy does not auto-detect
#      .trivyignore.yaml the way it does .trivyignore.
#   2. trivy-action's `trivyignores:` input mechanism (the only way to
#      pass --ignorefile through this action) concatenates every given
#      file's content into a NEW file it names "./trivyignores" - no
#      extension - before pointing TRIVY_IGNOREFILE at it. A yaml file
#      routed through that input would silently stop parsing as yaml the
#      moment it lands in that extension-less copy, and would then be
#      misread as (invalid, inert) plain-format lines: the scan would
#      report success while suppressing nothing. That failure mode is
#      worse than not having an ignore file at all, since it looks
#      handled.
# Relying on trivy's own default lookup instead sidesteps that mechanism
# entirely, and the plain format's line-level `exp:` suffix gives the
# same "don't suppress forever" property yaml's `expired_at` would have.
#
# SCOPE: this file is repo-root-global. Trivy's plain format matches by
# finding ID only - there is no per-package/per-image scoping available
# without yaml's `purls`/`paths` fields, which are off the table per
# above. Every entry names which image(s)/package(s) it covers today. If
# a future image introduces one of these same IDs in a package we COULD
# actually fix, this file would wrongly swallow it there too - check here
# first if a scan looks suspiciously clean.
#
# Every entry expires ~6 months from when it was added (2026-09-02) so a
# stale suppression makes CI fail loudly again instead of staying quiet
# forever by accident.

# --- images/seerr-mcp (@cyanheads/seerr-mcp-server's own npm tree) and
# --- images/claude-code (Claude Code's own npm tree) ---
# brace-expansion, ip-address, tar: transitive dependencies neither
# Dockerfile installs directly or can re-pin without forking the
# respective package.json. No non-breaking patched release resolvable
# through the existing top-level pins as of this review.
CVE-2026-14257 exp:2027-03-02
CVE-2026-69152 exp:2027-03-02
CVE-2026-69192 exp:2027-03-02
CVE-2026-73566 exp:2027-03-02

# --- images/truenas-mcp (Python, vendored inside pip itself, not our
# --- install line) ---
# msgpack 1.1.2 and setuptools 70.3.0 are NOT top-level packages our
# `pip install "mcp-proxy==0.12.0" "mcp<2.0.0"` line pulls in - `pip list`
# in the built image shows no such packages. Both are pip 26.2.1's own
# internal vendored copies (pip/_vendor/msgpack,
# pip/_vendor/setuptools via pip/_vendor/vendor.txt), present
# byte-for-byte in the untouched python:3.13.15-slim-bookworm base image
# before this Dockerfile runs anything - confirmed by scanning that base
# image directly. pip 26.2.1 is also the current latest pip release on
# PyPI, so there is no newer pip to bump to either. Tried
# `pip install --upgrade setuptools "msgpack>=1.2.1"` anyway (see
# images/truenas-mcp/Dockerfile) - it cannot reach pip's internal vendored
# copies and removed 0 of these findings, so it was left out.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 exp:2027-03-02
CVE-2025-47273 exp:2027-03-02

# --- images/truenas-mcp (Go, compiled into the truenas-mcp v0.0.6 binary
# --- TrueNAS itself publishes) ---
# golang.org/x/net v0.17.0 and Go stdlib v1.25.12 are baked into the
# prebuilt linux-amd64 binary fetched from truenas/truenas-mcp's GitHub
# release - this Dockerfile downloads and checksum-verifies that binary,
# it doesn't compile it, so its dependencies can't be bumped without
# building our own fork of an upstream project. v0.0.6 is upstream's
# current release.
